{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VGk6G12UipFrcEL75mkB8a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as the test bed. They derive formal specifications from C source code, generate Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verify parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline: extract, specify, synthesize, verify. Key components:\n- `cmd/` - Go utility implementations\n- `pkg/sys/` - Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Shared output formatting\n\n\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications\n\n**Current State:** Looking at what's already implemented, I can see we have the core infrastructure in place: differential testing utilities, syscall abstractions for both Darwin and Linux, formatting helpers for output, and the initial cat utility with its test suite.\n\n**Releases and status:** The foundational batch is spec-complete with code already present. The first release covering wc is done, followed by cat, sponge, and du implementations—all with their test suites. Now I'm checking the status of the version command and subsequent batches.\n\n**Upcoming work:** Batch 2 needs specifications for the trivial utilities like true, false, yes, basename, and dirname, while tee and head are also pending specs. There are deferred releases for ls core and extended functionality, plus ts, all with completed specs.\n\n**Current blockers:** I'm tracking an in-progress issue for writing the PRD for the true utility, and there's an orphaned PRD (prd019) that needs attention.\n\n**Next steps:** Since rel02.0 is the earliest incomplete release and issue 202 is already handling the true utility PRD, I should focus on writing the remaining PRDs for false, yes, and the other trivial utilities in that batch. Cases are all pending, so I need to prioritize PRDs first. Since issue 202 is already in progress for `true`'s PRD, the next logical task is writing the PRD for `false` — which is even simpler, just exiting with code 1. Following the naming convention, this would be prd021-false, and I should format it as a documentation issue.\n\nNow I'm structuring the PRD task with the reference implementation details and required reading materials...\n\nDefining the specific requirements sections... PRD covering false exit behavior: exits with status 1 regardless of arguments (derived from GNU coreutils false.c source). I also need to write requirements for flag handling where --help and --version print to stdout and exit cleanly, while other flags are silently ignored, plus stream behavior with proper SIGPIPE handling and stderr reserved for internal errors only. I'm mapping each requirement to acceptance criteria that can be verified through differential testing against gfalse. Following the structure from prd020-true.yaml as my template, using gfalse from Homebrew coreutils as the reference binary, and running all differential tests under LC_ALL=C. The acceptance criteria need to confirm the PRD file exists and is valid YAML, contains all required sections with uniquely-identified requirements that map to observable GNU false behavior, and that each criterion is verifiable through differential testing. I'm adjusting the required_reading to reference existing PRDs as pattern examples instead of relying on prd020-true, since \"orphaned PRD\" likely means the file exists but isn't properly linked in the roadmap. Let me write out the task cleanly now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":86465,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":86465},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3c151bc4-25c1-4526-8e22-6fdafae9e6ea","uuid":"313d4733-c726-4d3d-845e-ee3ce0b5c41f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VGk6G12UipFrcEL75mkB8a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment that derives formal specs from C source code, generates Go implementations via an AI orchestrator, and verifies parity against GNU reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with `cmd/` per-utility packages, shared `pkg/sys/` (syscalls), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `pkg/testutils/` (differential harness), and `magefiles/` (build targets).\n\n**Current state:** All code through rel01.3 exists in source: `pkg/testutils`, `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `cmd/wc`, `cmd/cat`, `cmd/sponge`, `cmd/du`, `cmd/version`. The next release is **rel02.0** (trivial utilities: true, false, yes, basename, dirname), which has **pending** spec status for all five use cases. Issue 202 is **in progress** for the `true` PRD (prd020-true).\n\n**Priority:** The earliest incomplete release is rel02.0. Issue 202 covers `true` (uc001). The next canonical task is the PRD for `false` (uc002), followed by `yes`, `basename`, `dirname`.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"prd021-false: Write PRD for false utility (rel02.0-uc002-false)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: documentation\n    format_rule: prd-format\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (components section -- cmd/ responsibility, DD4 reference binary convention, DD6 LC_ALL=C constraint)\n      - docs/VISION.yaml (success criteria SC1 differential test parity, SC2 PRD coverage)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd020-true.yaml (sibling trivial utility PRD -- follow its structure and granularity as the closest pattern)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd006-cat.yaml (established PRD pattern reference if prd020-true is unavailable)\n      - docs/road-map.yaml (rel02.0 scope and use case mapping)\n      - docs/utilities.yaml (false utility profile -- difficulty, memory model)\n\n    files:\n      - path: docs/specs/product-requirements/prd021-false.yaml\n        action: create\n        note: PRD for the GNU false utility covering exit behavior, flag handling, and stream behavior\n\n    required_sections:\n      - \"Problem: false is a coreutils utility that always exits with status 1, used in shell scripts for unconditional failure\"\n      - \"Goals: G1 -- match GNU false exit and flag behavior under LC_ALL=C via differential testing against gfalse\"\n      - \"Requirements: R1 exit behavior, R2 flag handling (--help, --version), R3 stream and signal behavior\"\n      - \"Non-Goals: what is out of scope for this trivial utility\"\n      - \"Acceptance Criteria: checkable outcomes mapped to each requirement, verifiable via differential testing\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Write PRD requirement for false exit behavior: exits with status 1 when invoked with no arguments, exits with status 1 when invoked with arbitrary non-flag arguments (GNU false ignores all operands). Derive from GNU coreutils false.c source.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Write PRD requirement for flag handling: --help writes usage information to stdout and exits 0, --version writes version string to stdout and exits 0. All other flags and arguments are silently ignored (GNU coreutils convention for trivial utilities). Derive from GNU coreutils false.c source.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Write PRD requirement for stream and signal behavior: SIGPIPE handling via pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler (ARCHITECTURE.yaml pkg/sys component), stderr used only for internal errors, stdout silent in normal operation.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Include acceptance criteria for each requirement that are verifiable via differential testing against the gfalse Homebrew reference binary (ARCHITECTURE.yaml DD4). Each acceptance criterion must specify the observable behavior being compared.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the PRD structure and granularity established by prd020-true.yaml as the closest sibling utility. If prd020-true.yaml is not available, follow prd006-cat.yaml structure.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Reference binary is gfalse from Homebrew coreutils (ARCHITECTURE.yaml DD4). All differential tests run under LC_ALL=C (ARCHITECTURE.yaml DD6).\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"The implementation target is cmd/false/main.go with differential tests in cmd/false/false_test.go, following the cmd/ package-per-utility pattern from ARCHITECTURE.yaml.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"File docs/specs/product-requirements/prd021-false.yaml exists and is valid YAML\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"PRD contains all required sections: Problem, Goals, Requirements, Non-Goals, and Acceptance Criteria\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Every requirement has a unique sequential ID (R1, R2, ...) and maps to observable GNU false behavior derived from C source analysis\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Acceptance criteria are verifiable via differential testing against gfalse and reference specific requirement IDs\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":86465,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":86465},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3c151bc4-25c1-4526-8e22-6fdafae9e6ea","uuid":"2d97ebbb-d627-4863-89c3-6a3416e73730"}
